WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.695
JavaScript and TypeScript applications still need runtime validation because external data arrives at runtime.

00:00:05.695 --> 00:00:08.493
TypeScript can help developers reason about code,

00:00:08.493 --> 00:00:14.088
but it does not prove that a request body, query parameter, route parameter, header,

00:00:14.088 --> 00:00:17.286
cookie, or webhook payload matches the expected shape.

00:00:17.286 --> 00:00:22.881
JSON parsing only proves that data is parseable, not that it is safe, authorized,

00:00:22.881 --> 00:00:24.080
complete, or meaningful.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:29.676
Schema validation gives teams a clear way to define expected fields, types, formats, ranges,

00:00:29.676 --> 00:00:30.475
and constraints.

00:00:30.475 --> 00:00:33.672
It can reject unknown fields, normalize allowed values,

00:00:33.672 --> 00:00:36.870
and keep invalid data away from business logic.

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:42.465
This is especially important in APIs where clients may send extra fields, nested objects,

00:00:42.465 --> 00:00:47.661
unexpected arrays, or values that look harmless until they reach a database query,

00:00:47.661 --> 00:00:49.659
file operation, or downstream service.

00:00:49.659 --> 00:00:53.256
Type confusion and mass assignment are common design risks.

00:00:53.256 --> 00:00:59.651
Type confusion happens when code assumes data has one type or shape but receives something else.

00:00:59.651 --> 00:01:05.246
Mass assignment happens when user-controlled fields can set properties that should not be user-controlled,

00:01:05.246 --> 00:01:08.843
such as roles, ownership, approval status, or internal flags.

00:01:08.843 --> 00:01:15.238
Validation should define both what is allowed and what must never be accepted from the client.

00:01:15.238 --> 00:01:19.634
Validation should happen before data reaches business logic, databases, files, commands,

00:01:19.634 --> 00:01:20.833
or downstream services.

00:01:20.833 --> 00:01:24.031
Error responses should be useful but not revealing.

00:01:24.031 --> 00:01:29.227
A client can be told that input failed validation without receiving stack traces,

00:01:29.227 --> 00:01:32.424
internal schema details, database messages, or implementation paths.

00:01:32.424 --> 00:01:37.220
Good validation improves security, reliability, and developer clarity at the same time.
